<section class="story-section" id="problem">
  <p class="eyebrow">01 / The problem before</p>
  <h2>Must every job need<br />another training set?</h2>
  <p>GPT-1 and BERT offered a useful recipe: learn from text first, then adapt to a job with labelled examples. <strong>Fine-tuning</strong> means updating a model’s adjustable numbers for that job. Want another task? Prepare another set of examples and train again.</p>
  <p>But ordinary writing already contains questions and answers, translations, and summaries. Could learning to continue that writing also teach a model something about those jobs?</p>
</section>
<section class="story-section" id="idea">
  <p class="eyebrow">02 / The big idea</p>
  <h2>The task can be text.</h2>
  <p>In 2019, GPT-2 explored this possibility with a larger Transformer trained on WebText, a collection of web pages. Its exercise stayed simple: predict the next <strong>token</strong>, a piece of text, using the tokens before it.</p>
  <p>A <strong>prompt</strong> is the text supplied before a continuation. End an article with <code>TL;DR:</code>—“too long; didn’t read”—and a summary becomes a plausible continuation. Supply a passage and a question ending in <code>A:</code>, and an answer can come next. The model’s numbers stay fixed; the prompt changes what comes next.</p>
  <p>This is <strong>zero-shot transfer</strong>: trying a task without training the model on a labelled dataset for that task. GPT-2 showed signs of several abilities this way, but its summaries could confuse details and its answers could be shallow. Continuing convincingly is not the same as answering correctly.</p>
