<section class="story-section" id="mattered">
  <p class="eyebrow">04 / Why it mattered</p>
  <h2>A prompt becomes an interface.</h2>
  <p>GPT-2 made a compelling case for testing a pretrained writer on different tasks through text alone. A summary and an answer could use the same model, with no new output layer or fine-tuning step.</p>
  <p>Larger models generally improved across several of the report’s tasks. That raises the next question: how predictably does more model, more data, or more training improve a language model?</p>
  <a class="next-chapter" href="/chapters/scaling-laws/"><span>Next · 2020</span><strong>Scaling laws ↗</strong><span>What changes when we make a language model bigger?</span></a>
</section>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="deeper">
  <summary>Go deeper <span>Objective, architecture, results & limits</span></summary>
  <div class="disclosure-body">
    <h3>One training objective, many prompts</h3>
    <p>For tokens x₁ through xₜ, training minimises <code>L = −Σₜ log Pθ(xₜ | x₁, …, xₜ₋₁)</code>. The adjustable model parameters θ change during training. During these task evaluations, θ stays fixed. The prompt supplies context to the same next-token distribution. Generating a whole answer repeats that prediction, appending each chosen token before predicting the next.</p>
    <p>The report’s zero-shot setting means no task-specific parameter updates. Some evaluations still use example pairs in the prompt, including translation and open-domain question answering. Reading comprehension includes conversation history. Prompt formatting, candidate scoring, and decoding choices also affect results.</p>
    <h3>A larger forward-reading Transformer</h3>
    <p>GPT-2 uses a decoder-style Transformer: attention can read earlier positions but cannot peek at future tokens. The largest model has roughly 1.5 billion parameters, 48 layers, and 1,600-dimensional internal states. Its context holds 1,024 tokens. A byte-based subword vocabulary contains 50,257 tokens; words can span multiple tokens.</p>
    <p>Compared with GPT-1, layer normalisation moves before each attention or feed-forward sub-block, with another normalisation at the end. Normalisation rescales internal activations. WebText contains slightly over eight million documents and 40 GB of text, extracted from web pages linked on Reddit, then cleaned and deduplicated. It is not a collection of Reddit comments.</p>
    <h3>What worked, and what did not?</h3>
    <p>On the CoQA reading-comprehension development set, GPT-2 scored 55 F1 without training on its question–answer pairs. F1 here measures overlap between predicted and reference answers. The report contrasts this with a supervised BERT system nearing 89 F1, and observes that GPT-2 sometimes relies on simple cues such as returning a name for a “who” question.</p>
    <p>For summarisation, the authors appended <code>TL;DR:</code>, sampled 100 tokens while choosing among the two most likely next tokens, and kept the first three sentences. Table 4 reports an average ROUGE score of 21.40, versus 20.98 for three randomly selected article sentences and 15.03 without the hint. ROUGE measures textual overlap with reference summaries. The hint helps, but the result is still weak.</p>
    <p>Training text and test passages can overlap; avoiding task-specific training does not guarantee unseen content. Larger models broadly improved several tasks, but not every score rose at every size. These experiments do not establish dependable instruction following or factual accuracy.</p>
    <h3>What the toy leaves out</h3>
    <p>The six invented snippets share one passage. The toy matches an edited cue to a fixed table, ignoring case and extra whitespace; an empty cue allows all six. The bars count available snippets by format. “Try another” cycles through them. No training occurs, and the table cannot generalise to new cues or passages. GPT-2 instead learns a token predictor from text and composes continuations token by token.</p>
  </div>
</details>
